WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.200
Welcome back to Building
Ticket-to-Machine.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.800
This is Chapter 6: drift
reconciliation, the kit's Build 3.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.400
Events tell you what changed.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.880
A reconciliation tells
you what is wrong now,

5
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.762
including what changed
while nobody was listening.

6
00:00:13.762 --> 00:00:18.057
The drift client joins the HR
extract, Microsoft Graph and

7
00:00:18.057 --> 00:00:22.799
SailPoint on the employee ID and
measures Book 3's drift classes.

8
00:00:22.799 --> 00:00:26.519
This chapter adds a comparison
of two runs, so a scheduled run

9
00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:30.462
raises only what's new, and shows
what has stopped being measured.

10
00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:33.080
Then a two-day lab on a laptop.

11
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.405
Why a reconciliation as well as events?

12
00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:42.405
Because events only tell
you about changes you heard.

13
00:00:42.405 --> 00:00:45.380
A missed event is drift
nobody ever raised.

14
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.945
The reconciliation
measures the state itself,

15
00:00:47.945 --> 00:00:50.905
so it catches what changed
while nobody was listening.

16
00:00:50.905 --> 00:00:52.930
But one run is one moment.

17
00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:56.770
Push every finding every night
and you lean on de-duplication,

18
00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.255
and nothing tells you what disappeared.

19
00:00:59.255 --> 00:01:02.577
So this chapter compares
runs, raises only what's new,

20
00:01:02.577 --> 00:01:07.730
and refuses to call something resolved
just because a run didn't measure it.

21
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.005
Here's the drift client.

22
00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:15.740
Its inputs are an HR extract, Graph users,

23
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.962
SailPoint identities and uncorrelated
accounts, and optionally the Entra

24
00:01:20.962 --> 00:01:25.355
directory audit; each can be a saved
file, which is how the labs run.

25
00:01:25.355 --> 00:01:27.584
It measures four of Book 3's classes,

26
00:01:27.584 --> 00:01:31.043
and an unauthorized write
only when it reads the audit.

27
00:01:31.043 --> 00:01:35.126
Credential and schema drift are
always reported as not computed,

28
00:01:35.126 --> 00:01:37.168
because they need other systems.

29
00:01:37.168 --> 00:01:40.268
Each finding's ID is a
hash of what it's about,

30
00:01:40.268 --> 00:01:43.280
so the same drift has
the same ID every run.

31
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.820
And pushing to ServiceNow
still needs the apply flag.

32
00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.300
The new tool compares two runs by finding

33
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.270
ID and sorts every
finding into three groups.

34
00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:57.795
New ones should be raised.

35
00:01:57.795 --> 00:02:02.520
Persisting ones are already raised,
and de-duplication just adds a counter.

36
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.056
Resolved ones are no longer measured,

37
00:02:05.056 --> 00:02:08.082
which is a lead for the
re-read, never a closure.

38
00:02:08.082 --> 00:02:11.907
And it refuses to compare runs
that computed different classes.

39
00:02:11.907 --> 00:02:14.526
If one run read the audit
and the next didn't,

40
00:02:14.526 --> 00:02:17.444
every unauthorized write
would look resolved.

41
00:02:17.444 --> 00:02:20.669
A resolved finding always
prints with a reminder

42
00:02:20.669 --> 00:02:24.010
that only Chapter 5's
re-read closes its record.

43
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.935
To exercise the comparison, the chapter
adds a second day to the fictional estate.

44
00:02:32.935 --> 00:02:37.297
On day one, HR says Erin works
in HR, but someone has already

45
00:02:37.297 --> 00:02:42.123
typed Finance into Entra ID by
hand, and SailPoint still says HR.

46
00:02:42.123 --> 00:02:45.859
On day two, HR corrects
her department to Finance.

47
00:02:45.859 --> 00:02:51.210
Now Graph agrees with HR, but SailPoint,
not yet re-aggregated, doesn't.

48
00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.869
The chapter also adds a fixture
settings file with no secrets,

49
00:02:54.869 --> 00:02:58.750
and the client now creates its
output folder if it's missing.

50
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.994
Four new tests cover the day-two story,

51
00:03:04.994 --> 00:03:08.213
identical runs, the refusal
and the command line.

52
00:03:08.213 --> 00:03:12.555
The kit's original thirteen drift
tests still pass, including the check

53
00:03:12.555 --> 00:03:16.750
that the Python and PowerShell
clients produce the same finding IDs.

54
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:21.750
One caution: these run over fictional
exports, and two inputs, the manager

55
00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:27.250
expansion and the SailPoint employee ID
attribute, are still on the verify list.

56
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:31.287
And expect a burst of High findings
in a real estate's first month.

57
00:03:31.287 --> 00:03:34.050
Book 3 calls that the measurement working.

58
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.250
The facts the join depends
on, read on November first.

59
00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.225
Graph lists users with
User dot Read dot All.

60
00:03:44.225 --> 00:03:47.963
For the manager, Learn documents
expanding it on the users list,

61
00:03:47.963 --> 00:03:52.563
while the manager endpoint itself
doesn't support application permissions.

62
00:03:52.563 --> 00:03:57.874
The audit needs AuditLog dot Read dot All
and is available in the government clouds.

63
00:03:57.874 --> 00:04:03.150
SailPoint identities come from Search,
paged past ten thousand, and uncorrelated

64
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:08.037
accounts from the Accounts API with a
filter, at most two hundred fifty a page.

65
00:04:08.037 --> 00:04:11.630
And GCC uses the worldwide Graph host.

66
00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.017
Three inputs aren't settled.

67
00:04:17.017 --> 00:04:20.918
Whether expanding the manager works
under an application permission.

68
00:04:20.918 --> 00:04:24.729
Which SailPoint identity
attribute holds the HR key,

69
00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:28.193
the employee number or an
employee ID attribute.

70
00:04:28.193 --> 00:04:31.403
And the exact property names
the Entra audit uses for

71
00:04:31.403 --> 00:04:34.468
the changes the client
watches, such as department.

72
00:04:34.468 --> 00:04:36.554
Each is on the kit's verify list.

73
00:04:36.554 --> 00:04:40.407
Lab 2 records whether Graph
rejects the manager expansion,

74
00:04:40.407 --> 00:04:44.550
and Chapter 3's lab finds where
your tenant keeps the HR key.

75
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.463
What this proves.

76
00:04:49.463 --> 00:04:55.081
Every active HR record has its accounts,
every terminated one has none enabled,

77
00:04:55.081 --> 00:04:59.835
and every enabled account belongs
to a person: account management,

78
00:04:59.835 --> 00:05:04.762
AC-2, and Book 3's leaver and orphan
measures, kept as each run's output.

79
00:05:04.762 --> 00:05:09.215
Drift is measured on a schedule, and
what changed between runs is reported:

80
00:05:09.215 --> 00:05:12.062
continuous monitoring
and system monitoring.

81
00:05:12.062 --> 00:05:15.766
And what wasn't measured is stated,
not implied, which feeds the

82
00:05:15.766 --> 00:05:20.124
Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
13's detection coverage measure.

83
00:05:20.124 --> 00:05:24.070
The not-computed list and the
refusal are that evidence.

84
00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:28.870
Who's involved?

85
00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:33.565
Developers and identity engineers who
schedule the reconciliation and set the

86
00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:37.489
estate's settings: which SailPoint
attribute holds the employee ID,

87
00:05:37.489 --> 00:05:41.483
which sources are authoritative,
and which apps are allowed to write.

88
00:05:41.483 --> 00:05:45.770
And the identity data steward, who
reads the output, triages the first

89
00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:50.433
month's burst by class and severity,
and sends decisions to the right people.

90
00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:53.411
The client reads only
what Chapter 3 granted:

91
00:05:53.411 --> 00:05:58.840
Graph user and audit reads with a
certificate, and two SailPoint read scopes.

92
00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:04.728
Lab 1 tells the two-day story.

93
00:06:04.728 --> 00:06:06.466
Seventeen tests pass.

94
00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:11.777
Day one: run the reconciliation over
all the fixtures, including the audit.

95
00:06:11.777 --> 00:06:17.047
It counts four attribute drifts, two
missing identities, three orphan accounts,

96
00:06:17.047 --> 00:06:20.160
two stale accesses and
one unauthorized write,

97
00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.753
and lists credential and
schema drift as not computed.

98
00:06:23.753 --> 00:06:28.164
In the CSV, Erin's Entra department
is Finance where HR says HR,

99
00:06:28.164 --> 00:06:32.664
and the audit shows a help desk
administrator made the change.

100
00:06:32.664 --> 00:06:35.465
Carol, terminated, is still enabled.

101
00:06:35.465 --> 00:06:39.978
Day two, after HR's correction:
exactly the same counts.

102
00:06:39.978 --> 00:06:42.080
The totals hide what moved.

103
00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:47.430
Now compare the two days.

104
00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.718
One new, eleven persisting, one resolved.

105
00:06:50.718 --> 00:06:54.414
Entra ID now matches HR, so
that finding is resolved,

106
00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:59.343
but SailPoint still holds the old
value, so a new finding opens there.

107
00:06:59.343 --> 00:07:02.554
And notice what persists:
the unauthorized write.

108
00:07:02.554 --> 00:07:05.778
HR agreeing afterwards
doesn't make a hand-typed

109
00:07:05.778 --> 00:07:08.518
change authorized; a person decides that.

110
00:07:08.518 --> 00:07:11.931
Last, run day two without
the audit and compare.

111
00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:14.805
The tool refuses, exiting with an error.

112
00:07:14.805 --> 00:07:19.320
Without that refusal, the unauthorized
write would have looked resolved.

113
00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:27.463
Lab 2 runs the client live, on a
test tenant and a SailPoint sandbox

114
00:07:27.463 --> 00:07:31.857
with synthetic data, never production;
the authors haven't run it.

115
00:07:31.857 --> 00:07:34.146
Grant only Chapter 3's reads.

116
00:07:34.146 --> 00:07:37.340
Write a small HR extract
that matches test users,

117
00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:42.132
with one terminated user still enabled
and one department that differs.

118
00:07:42.132 --> 00:07:45.370
Run the client, and check its
counts against a hand count.

119
00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:48.558
Record whether Graph rejects
the manager expansion.

120
00:07:48.558 --> 00:07:52.446
Run again the next day after changing
one department, and compare.

121
00:07:52.446 --> 00:07:53.357
Push nothing.

122
00:07:53.357 --> 00:07:55.380
Then delete everything.

123
00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.155
The dangers.

124
00:08:00.155 --> 00:08:04.418
A class one run skipped would look
resolved, so unauthorized writes

125
00:08:04.418 --> 00:08:08.605
would vanish the night the audit
read fails; the refusal stops that.

126
00:08:08.605 --> 00:08:11.144
A resolved finding must
never close a record,

127
00:08:11.144 --> 00:08:15.055
because drift can leave the
measurement without leaving the estate.

128
00:08:15.055 --> 00:08:19.967
Pushing every finding every night fills
the tracker, so push only what's new.

129
00:08:19.967 --> 00:08:23.292
The first month's burst
isn't failure; triage it.

130
00:08:23.292 --> 00:08:27.317
Guests and the HR source's own
accounts are excluded as noise.

131
00:08:27.317 --> 00:08:32.600
And an HR extract is personal data:
keep it where HR data is allowed.

132
00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:37.550
Done looks like this.

133
00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:41.412
The HR extract's statuses map
the way the client expects.

134
00:08:41.412 --> 00:08:43.425
The three estate settings are set.

135
00:08:43.425 --> 00:08:46.475
A live run matched a hand
count on synthetic data.

136
00:08:46.475 --> 00:08:51.813
The scheduled job compares each run with
the last and pushes only new findings.

137
00:08:51.813 --> 00:08:56.262
Every run computes the same classes,
or the comparison is rerun.

138
00:08:56.262 --> 00:09:00.712
And the tests pass, with the PowerShell
client's IDs still matching.

139
00:09:00.712 --> 00:09:06.700
Next, Chapter 7 turns closed findings
into the evidence an I S S O keeps.

140
00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:07.860
Thanks for watching.
